{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and of renaming requirements.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed test is adequate.\n\n**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refactor` has two parts:\n1. The `habeas-refactor` skill states in full how to move code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node.\n2. The same skill states in full how to rename requirements. Under `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, each topic has exactly one home skill.\n\n**Coverage.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_home_of_moves_and_requirement_renames]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It checks three things.\n- **Moves.** It asserts the `## Workflow` section of the refactor skill's `SKILL.md` contains these phrases:\n  - \"move the `{req}` directive to its new chapter\";\n  - the file or directory mapping, `--map src/config.rs=src/settings.rs`;\n  - the node mapping, `--map`;\n  - the definition mapping, `--map requirements.cli.check=requirements.core.check`.\n\n  Together these cover moving files, code nodes, and requirements. The `section` helper limits the search to the Workflow body, so the phrases cannot be satisfied from elsewhere in the file.\n- **Renames.** `assert_phrases(\"habeas-refactor\", [\"Renaming a requirement's ID is a definition mapping\"])` asserts the rename guidance is in the skill. I read `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md` to confirm the Gotchas section contains it.\n- **Exclusive home.** `assert_home(\"habeas-refactor\")` asserts that every `home_markers` phrase for the refactor skill appears in it. Those phrases are `habeas refactor --dry-run`, the node-mapping syntax, and the \"Would leave ... unchanged\" explanation. It then asserts that none of those phrases appears in any other bundled skill, across each skill's `SKILL.md` and `references/` files via `bundled_skill`. A failure message names the skill that restates one. This is a real assertion of the \"exactly one skill\" obligation.\n\n**Supporting tests.** Other tests in the file cover the referral side of the home rule:\n- `bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it` checks that other skills refer to habeas-refactor.\n- `the_habeas_skill_routes_each_task_to_the_skill_that_covers_it` checks the routing table.\n- `the_requirements_skill_renames_requirements_through_refactor_before_editing` checks that the requirements skill sends renames to habeas-refactor.\n\nThese are not marked for this subject, but they reinforce it.\n\n**Limitations.** The exclusivity check uses only three marker phrases. The rename sentence and the file and definition mapping examples are asserted present in the refactor skill but not asserted absent from the others. A restatement of only those pieces elsewhere would not be caught. That is a thin spot, not a missing obligation. The topic is the refactor procedure, and its distinctive procedure phrases are checked for exclusivity.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gap.",
